> tl;dr
>
> - 79.9% of visitors report sites are helpful (+7 percentage points YoY; n=35,113 responses, +4.3% YoY volume).
> - Post‑migration stability recovered from 6 to ~4.5 daily page errors; trending toward pre‑migration baseline of ~4.
> - Median response time improved to 0.45s from 0.88s on legacy.
> - Achieved through coordinated work across platform, product and editorial teams with clear ownership and cadence.

After migrating to Azure, daily page failures increased from ~4 to ~6—users reported timeouts during peak traffic, and perceived stability declined. We instrumented better, created a risk register, and focused on fixes with the most user impact.

The recovery: fewer page failures, faster responses, and a visible path back to baseline reliability. We kept listening to users throughout to ensure the numbers matched their experience.

## Problem

A major infrastructure migration created instability and perception risk: error frequency increased and perceived usefulness could decline despite strategic investments.

## Approach

- Focused reliability backlog prioritized by impact; added a live risk register to guide mitigation.
- Hardened monitoring; categorized failures by root cause and owner.
- Iterated on caching, CDN, and DB tuning to reduce tail latency.
- Closed the loop with users via on‑site feedback to validate progress.

## Outcomes

- Usefulness: 79.9% report sites are helpful (+7pp YoY; 35,113 responses, +4.3% YoY volume).
- Stability: average daily page failures declined from 6 (first six months) to ~4.5 (last three months), approaching ~4 pre‑migration.
- Speed: response time improved to 0.45s from 0.88s legacy.

